
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that settles it. We're designing [1msub-project 1: tasks + storage[0m, local-only, with the data shapes deliberately
  kept serializable and event-emitting so notifications (sub-project 2 and 3) hook in without a rewrite. I'll come back
  to notifications as its own spec once this exists.

  Next question, one at a time. Since single-user notifications are mostly [3mtime[0m-driven (due soon, overdue, recurring)
  rather than [3mactor[0m-driven, whether tasks carry due dates isn't a cosmetic field choice — it decides whether sub-project
  2 has anything to observe at all. So I want to pin the v1 task feature set.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much task model exists before we stop and build. Every field is cheap to add now and annoying to
  retrofit once there's stored data in people's browsers (you'd need a migration), but every field is also scope.

  - [1mMinimal[0m — title, done/not-done, created-at. Fastest to working. Notifications later would have almost nothing to
    fire on, so sub-project 2 would likely force a model change anyway.
  - [1mMinimal + due dates[0m — adds a due date and a completed-at. Small addition, and it's exactly what makes time-based
    notifications possible without touching the model again.
  - [1mFuller[0m — adds priority, tags/lists, notes, and a general edit surface. More useful as an actual app, meaningfully
    more UI, and more of it is guesswork about what you'd want.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m minimal + due dates. It's roughly one extra field and one extra input, and it's the difference
  between sub-project 2 being a design exercise and being buildable.
